00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.020
It was funny, when I was doing

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.320
what light research I do before this,

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.670
I realized that we are the exact same age,

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.040
and that created a bit of
an existential moment in me,

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.870
because you run a large, multinational,

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.900
like 100,000-plus person organization,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.180
and you know, I hang out in a
walk-in closet making videos,

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.833
so it kind a made me ponder my life.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.500
- But no, not at all, you've touched

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.760
hundreds of millions of
children all around the world,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.173
it's extraordinary work.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.280
- But, I'd love to hear,

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.470
one of the things that we like to do,

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.470
and I encourage anyone who's
watching on YouTube or Facebook

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.980
to start asking questions,
about really anything,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.730
but I think what I like to do here,

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.450
especially because there's a
lot of young people watching,

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.390
is just understand, how do you become,

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.450
well first, what does Novartis do?

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.660
I think many people know

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.493
it's one of the largest

00:02:46.493 --> 00:02:48.510
pharmaceutical companies in the world,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.560
but also, and what's it focus,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.020
but also, I'd like to understand
how you ended up here.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.033
So maybe just start with,

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:55.970
just make sure everyone understands

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.400
what Novartis does, and what its focus is,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.753
and then I'd love to go
into your background.

00:02:59.753 --> 00:03:01.320
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.710
So, Novartis is about
a 250 year old company,

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:08.880
beginning in the world of
dye-making in the 1700s,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.430
chemicals in the 1800s,
pharmaceuticals in the 1900s.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:18.410
Today, we're one of the 50
largest companies in the world,

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.220
one of the two or three

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.230
largest makers of medicines in the world.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:28.020
We make about, on the order of 800,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.220
we reach about 800 million patients a year

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.490
with our medicines,

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.724
we produce about 70 billion
doses a year of medicine

00:03:34.724 --> 00:03:37.350
in about 150 countries.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.230
Everything ranging from medicines
to tackle heart disease,

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.044
and lung disease, and cancer,

00:03:43.044 --> 00:03:46.830
all the way to malaria,
and leprosy medicine.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.430
But also, very high-end
technologies like gene therapies,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.930
and cell therapies.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.914
We're also the second largest manufacturer

00:03:53.914 --> 00:03:56.850
of generic medicines in the world.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:01.850
So, a big goal to impact public health,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.360
and as a public health physician,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.340
it's really a dream job
to have this opportunity.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.730
As for myself, my parents
were Indian immigrants,

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:17.730
they came to the U.S. in the
late '60s and the early 1970s.

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:21.450
I grew up in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania,

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.330
where I went to wonderful
public schools there,

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:28.580
and then later in southern New Jersey,

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.040
and so very grateful for
the incredible education,

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.470
and very dedicated,
amazing parents that I had.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.700
I went to the University of
Chicago, studied biology,

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.310
went to Harvard Medical
School, studied medicine.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.620
Did a lot of work in developing countries.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.198
My passion for public
health was in Africa,

00:04:44.198 --> 00:04:48.130
working in Africa, but
also in Peru, India,

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.120
and a few other locations.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.940
But through kind of twists and turns,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.220
I ended up wanting to pursue
the private sector approach

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.810
to impacting health on a global scale.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.450
Was very interested in the science

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.610
of developing drugs and medicines,

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:04.670
and how that could have a big impact.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.920
And so, I came to Novartis in 2005,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.850
spent most of my career
developing vaccines and drugs.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.871
I'm a member of the
National Academy of Medicine

00:05:14.871 --> 00:05:17.630
in the United States because
of the work I've done

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.473
developing drugs and vaccines.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.140
And that led, from one thing or another,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.840
to me somehow being the
CEO of this company,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.300
as a physician/scientist who
always just had a big dream

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.350
to impact the public health of the world.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:34.340
- Thanks for that, and
there's a lot there.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:36.360
I'm assuming most people
have heard of Novartis,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.310
but just the numbers that you just said

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.630
were kind of mind-boggling.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:42.750
I mean, it's probably safe to say, maybe,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.620
everyone watching, or at
least a close relative

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.310
of everyone watching is probably,

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.250
in some way, shape, or form,

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.180
taking some kind of medication,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:51.013
hopefully being helped

00:05:51.013 --> 00:05:53.390
by some type of medication
that's a Novartis drug.

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.670
So, that's incredible, incredible reach.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:00.040
One question, actually, there's
a question from YouTube,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.347
Annam Khan, no relation,
as far as I'm aware,

00:06:03.347 --> 00:06:04.980
"How did you know what path

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.220
and what career was best for you?

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.540
How did you know it was your passion?"

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.490
Because you started as a,
you went to medical school,

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:10.593
you are a physician.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:12.630
Did at some point it switch,

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:13.590
at first you thought you were going to be

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.217
a practicing physician,
but then you thought,

00:06:15.217 --> 00:06:17.220
"Hey, what if I could help develop drugs,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.467
or help scale therapies, whatever?"

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:21.970
- I think my mother is still wondering

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:23.400
when I'll become a cardiologist,

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.882
but I think she's generally

00:06:24.882 --> 00:06:29.190
has moved on from that discussion.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:30.640
Yeah, I don't exactly know.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.710
People often ask me, and
I actually just completed

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.630
reading a great book by
David Epstein called "Range,"

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.240
and it talks about how, in the end,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.785
building broad ranges of experience

00:06:41.785 --> 00:06:45.450
will allow you to discover
where your best fit is.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.030
So I think probably for ten years,

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.467
I did my medical studies,

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:50.970
I had every intention
of becoming a physician,

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:53.780
I worked at the World Health Organization,

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:56.420
worked quite a bit in
public health in Africa,

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:58.050
some great leaders.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.990
Took a plunge into the private sector

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:01.610
without knowing where it would go.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:03.250
I would say there were
many twists and turns,

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.750
and many moments I would have thought

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.160
this was not a good decision.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.900
But then, I ended up, actually,
in the world of vaccines,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.130
and I loved vaccines,

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.860
I loved developing new
vaccines for children.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:14.750
Again, had the opportunity

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:17.730
to work with a number of
extraordinary vaccines,

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.910
and again, one thing led to another.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.740
I think, the book talks about it,

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.637
I think it's worth remembering
in the world of education,

00:07:26.637 --> 00:07:28.660
we wanna build broad minds,

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.140
because the world is complex,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.612
and I think building
more range in yourself,

00:07:32.612 --> 00:07:36.760
from the disciplines you learn,
how you can solve problems,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.320
how you can think,

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.130
is what will ultimately
make you successful,

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.283
and I think Khan Academy
builds that in people.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:46.213
- And of course, you know,

00:07:46.213 --> 00:07:48.040
there's a kind of, I guess, a very blunt,

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.680
or funny version of the
question I was going to ask,

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.400
that someone at YouTube, AZ, asked,

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.110
when he said, how did you
take over the company,

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:57.610
which I don't think you perceived
your job as (indistinct).

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:01.000
But I guess, I'll take a
different version of it.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.160
What do you think, you know,

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:05.840
when I was a kid, and
saw someone become a CEO

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.990
of a multinational
organization like Novartis,

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:09.500
and I'd be like, "How did they do that?"

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.950
There's a lot of smart
people in the world,

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:13.670
there's hundreds of thousands, and yeah,

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:16.560
I think at Novartis, you have
100,000-something employees,

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.800
what do you think it was about you,

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.313
and either it was opportunities you got,

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.133
serendipity, and things
that you actively did

00:08:26.133 --> 00:08:27.883
that kind of got you on that path?

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:30.800
- Yeah, I'd say a couple things.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:34.385
I give tremendous credit
to my parents, and my wife,

00:08:34.385 --> 00:08:36.438
the three of whom without which

00:08:36.438 --> 00:08:39.490
I certainly would not
be here at this moment.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.900
But I think it was a mixture

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.730
of being able to understand
the science of medicines,

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:45.880
and having a deep understanding

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:48.330
of human biology and science,

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.570
combined with having learned
how to translate that

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.360
into business, and
numbers, and financials,

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.288
and how does that translate into business,

00:08:56.288 --> 00:08:59.090
and really be able to
speak multiple languages,

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:00.910
speak the language of science,

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:04.570
and speak the language of business.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:06.130
But I think that the third element in it,

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:08.400
it came back to my education
at the University of Chicago,

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.570
where I was educated in the great books,

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:14.470
was a kind of a deep understanding
of purpose, and values,

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:19.470
and mission, and trying to
instill in an organization,

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:21.100
and I think that combination.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.910
I knew when I was named CEO,

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:25.410
I was not the traditional choice.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.400
I think our board took a huge chance

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:28.510
in making me a CEO.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:31.990
There was a lot more traditional
choices one could take

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:33.670
than taking a physician/scientist

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.400
who mostly worked on
developing drugs and vaccines.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.560
If I'm not mistaken, I'm only one

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.980
of maybe a handful in the whole
biopharmaceutical industry

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:46.120
who comes from more the R&amp;D background.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:47.280
But I think it was that mix,

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.270
and having that ability

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:50.740
to cross those different disciplines,

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:55.280
and having a very humanitarian
approach, or view,

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:56.900
and we have a long journey to get there

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:57.733
to actually realize that,

00:09:57.733 --> 00:10:00.963
but that was, I think,
the kind of key elements.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:04.613
- Yeah, and given,
obviously, Novartis itself,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:06.860
a major pharmaceutical company,

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.630
but your background as a physician,

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.250
you've helped develop vaccines.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:12.410
The obvious questions, you could imagine,

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.300
a lot of questions coming in
on social media about that.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:18.100
Judy Lenahan, "When will a vaccine

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:19.630
for COVID-19 become available,

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:22.890
and what unanticipated
roadblocks might delay it?"

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:26.217
And then Muran Ararat
from Facebook is asking,

00:10:26.217 --> 00:10:27.780
"If and when it gets developed,

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:32.630
how long does it take for mass
production and availability?"

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.026
What's your view on that?

00:10:34.026 --> 00:10:36.280
I don't know if Novartis is
explicitly doing activity there,

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.660
but I'm sure you also have a general view

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.460
of what's going on in the ecosystem.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.260
- Yeah, you know, I spent nine years,

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:44.560
eight, nine years developing vaccines,

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.230
and working hard in that space.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.980
We ultimately sold our vaccines business,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:50.720
so we're not in the world
of vaccines right now,

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.260
but I can offer the perspective

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:55.023
more of somebody who knows
the space extremely well.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.030
I think it's very important to understand

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.680
what it takes to develop a vaccine,

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:02.940
and how different a
vaccine is from a drug.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.950
In the case of a drug, we
know a patient has a disease,

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:07.400
and we're treating the disease

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:09.633
so the so-called risk-benefit equation

00:11:09.633 --> 00:11:11.980
is highly favored towards the drug,

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:14.690
because we know the patient is ill.

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.530
In the case of a vaccine,
we're immunizing somebody

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.160
who is otherwise healthy,

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:21.940
so the bar for safety is extremely high,

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:25.480
and the bar for what the
benefit of the vaccine is

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.780
is also extremely high.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:29.760
The FDA recently put out guidance

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.960
which actually was very much
in line with what I expected.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:35.710
The FDA said they expect studies

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:38.600
to be very large and well-controlled,

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:40.940
and said that studies should likely have

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:43.840
thousands, if not tens
of thousands of patients.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.540
They expected a vaccine to
have 50% efficacy at least,

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:50.370
and they expected, at minimum,

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:51.760
six months of follow up

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.490
for the patients who are in the trial,

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:55.900
and longer if there was potentially

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.490
a new technology that's being used.

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:00.050
All of that comes together,

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:01.900
and I've been one of the people
who consistently have said,

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:03.990
that, being realistic,

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:07.730
given that any drug you
put into the clinic,

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:09.100
any vaccine you put in the clinic,

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.270
has somewhere between a one,

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.820
and at best case, 10% chance of making it.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:16.950
So if we put in 100, we
probably will get one.

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.870
If we really understand what
we're doing, if we put in ten,

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:23.490
we'll get one really
effective vaccine or drug.

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:28.050
That's just the historical
odds in our sector.

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:28.930
And then you combine that

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:30.620
with what the FDA just talked abut,

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:34.010
I think it's realistic to expect
a broadly-available vaccine

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:37.900
not before towards the
latter part of next year.

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.920
Now, could there be an upside case,

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:41.280
could everything work perfectly?

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:42.710
Of course, and I understand

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.970
that's a lot of where the discussion is.

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:46.560
But it's important, also, to understand

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.630
why we want a very safe vaccine,

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.681
and why it's so important, in my mind,

00:12:50.681 --> 00:12:52.560
to maintain the public trust.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.890
Probably over the last 100 years,

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:58.178
vaccines, sanitation, and
a handful of medicines

00:12:58.178 --> 00:13:01.840
have led to the remarkable
gains in life expectancy,

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:03.850
where people, on average, in 1900,

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:05.550
lived to about 30 years of age,

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:08.150
and today, depending on
where you are in the world,

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:10.020
80 to 90 years of age.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:10.960
And what we don't wanna do

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.930
is undermine that belief in vaccines,

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:15.530
that's so fundamentally important

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:17.887
for our public health system.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:21.180
- And there's several really
powerful things you said.

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:23.990
I mean, one, I thought that
was a really interesting way

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:27.240
to compare normal pharmaceutical
drugs with a vaccine.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:30.040
As you mentioned, for
regular drug development,

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:32.880
someone has a disease, maybe
a really serious disease,

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:34.210
so you know they have it,

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:35.480
and so you just have to see, hey,

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:38.750
is the benefit of the drug,
probablistically, better

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:41.090
than the side effects, or the risk.

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:43.055
But a vaccine is a healthy person,

00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:46.240
and the side effects and the negatives,

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:48.540
these things, you have to
look over thousands of people,

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:52.040
because even a small
negative is not so good,

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:55.070
because you're giving
it to healthy people.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:58.530
And you also said, the expectation

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.350
is probably end of next calendar year,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:04.730
so end of 2021 is your best guess?

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:07.810
- It's my guess, it's
just one person's guess.

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:09.770
As you know, there are a
lot of opinions out there.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:12.270
But I think that would be remarkable,

00:14:12.270 --> 00:14:14.800
to have a brand new virus show up,

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:18.694
and to have a vaccine out
the door within two years,

00:14:18.694 --> 00:14:21.697
is frankly unprecedented,

00:14:21.697 --> 00:14:24.840
and it would not have happened,
as far as I know, before.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:27.470
In most cases, in pandemics,

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:29.410
we've generally had influenza pandemics,

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:32.490
so we've known how to
manufacture influenza vaccines

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.480
since about the 1950s,

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:37.710
at scale, very established technology.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.910
The only thing we're changing
is the type of virus,

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:43.100
we're not changing the
manufacturing platform,

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:45.341
we're not changing the nature
of the immune response.

00:14:45.341 --> 00:14:46.994
Now with COVID, we don't know

00:14:46.994 --> 00:14:49.620
what kind of immune response we need,

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:52.100
we're trying to use very
new technology platforms

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:55.040
that have never been
used in a large scale,

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.040
to navigate all that
complexity within two years

00:14:58.040 --> 00:14:59.783
would be pretty remarkable.

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:04.940
- So you're saying it's kind
of a fairly bullish prediction

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:05.830
to get end of,

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:08.079
because at least when you watch the news,

00:15:08.079 --> 00:15:11.230
and even if you hear
governments around the world,

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:13.870
it seems like people are
hopeful for three months,

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:16.250
or four months, or by January,

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:19.790
but it sounds like we should
probably set our expectations,

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:22.340
it would be a good outcome
if we have a vaccine

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:26.273
by November, December of 2021.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:30.490
- I would say yes, I think,

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:33.943
but that's not necessarily,
understandably,

00:15:33.943 --> 00:15:35.555
what people are hearing.

00:15:35.555 --> 00:15:37.160
I understand why there's hope,

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:38.980
and perhaps you could find a vaccine

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:40.890
that you could use with
certain populations,

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:42.940
first responders, faster,

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:46.550
but it's important to
understand what's required

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:48.100
from the data, and the science.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.738
And the science and the
data have to demonstrate,

00:15:50.738 --> 00:15:52.646
you're not gonna cause harm

00:15:52.646 --> 00:15:56.010
to people who receive the vaccine,

00:15:56.010 --> 00:16:00.303
and you need to have a reasonable
belief of strong efficacy.

00:16:01.339 --> 00:16:04.650
I spent a lot of time
developing pediatric vaccines,

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:08.690
as a simple thing like a
high fever, as an example.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:12.400
If you have a high fever,
in some significant rate,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.030
in 20%, 30% of children
who receive your vaccine,

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:18.280
and if you give that
vaccine to tens of millions

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:19.980
or hundreds of millions of people,

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.220
you suddenly have lots of people

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:22.950
showing up in the emergency room

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:25.050
with fever related to the vaccine,

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:25.883
which then leads to

00:16:25.883 --> 00:16:28.810
all other adverse
consequences for the system.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.590
So it's important when we ensure

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:33.610
that we have a vaccine
we really believe in,

00:16:33.610 --> 00:16:37.126
from an efficacy and safety standpoint.

00:16:37.126 --> 00:16:39.180
And I think experts in vaccines

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.630
probably generally have
that kind of perspective.

00:16:43.322 --> 00:16:45.290
- And just to make sure
everyone's on the same page,

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:46.760
the whole principle of immunization

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:49.190
is to inject something into your system

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:51.320
that you can build your
own immune response

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.010
so that if the real thing shows up,

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:55.760
so to speak, it's ready.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:58.390
And there's these notions of antibodies,

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:00.625
which students can learn
about on Khan Academy,

00:17:00.625 --> 00:17:03.420
which are kind of like tags,

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:06.920
or certain foreign substances
that your body doesn't want.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:09.050
And once things are tagged,

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:12.500
then your immune system can
go and clean up that stuff,

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:13.333
kill that stuff.

00:17:13.333 --> 00:17:15.963
And so you want those
tags, those antibodies.

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:18.540
How is a vaccine developed?

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:19.660
Is it essentially, do you get antibodies,

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:21.460
do you observe antibodies in people

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.560
who already have the
virus, you know, COVID,

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:25.440
and you take some of those,

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:27.180
do you just inject them in someone else?

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:28.940
Do you kind of denature them a little bit,

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:30.980
but the proteins are still there?

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:31.930
How does that work?

00:17:33.107 --> 00:17:34.823
- So the few things you can do.

00:17:36.388 --> 00:17:37.520
What you just referred to, Sal,

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:40.380
is so-called convalescent (indistinct).

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:43.410
These are patients who
have had the disease,

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:44.420
develop antibodies,

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.210
you take the antibodies
from those patients,

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:48.580
you give it to patients affected by COVID,

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:51.000
and you hope that transferring
the antibodies can help.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:53.640
There've been mixed
results so far with that,

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:54.910
there's a lot of complexity

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:56.700
in really ensuring you have an adequate

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:58.498
so-called dose of the antibody.

00:17:58.498 --> 00:18:00.920
So we'll have to see if that bears out,

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:03.720
but that's of course
not a scalable solution.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:07.010
So then the question is, how do you give

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:11.010
components of the coronavirus,
COVID-19, to a human,

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:14.530
to generate a very robust immune response?

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.970
An immune response that
has a few characteristics.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:20.670
One, that it has a high
enough level of antibodies,

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:21.503
as you've mentioned,

00:18:21.503 --> 00:18:24.160
that can prevent, hopefully,

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:27.210
the disease from taking hold in a person,

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:30.450
and hopefully also elicit
what's called immune memory.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:33.700
So immune memory is, the
immune system has the ability

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:37.270
to store cells, memory T-cells,

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:40.870
which can then come back if
the infection were to happen

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.420
at a later point in time.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.440
This allows for the vaccine to protect

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:46.440
over a long period of time.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:47.430
Now, right now there's a number

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:49.200
of different vaccine technologies

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:53.330
that are being used to
try to solve this problem.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:57.860
One is giving RNA, and so
for the students listening,

00:18:57.860 --> 00:19:00.860
you know DNA goes to RNA goes to protein.

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:04.660
So the idea here is if
you give patients RNA,

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:07.090
the cells in the body will turn that RNA

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:09.410
into the COVID surface proteins,

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:12.110
so one of the proteins on
the surface of the virus,

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:14.400
and then an immune response will develop.

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:18.197
Other approaches use DNA,
and the similar sort of idea,

00:19:18.197 --> 00:19:21.860
can you get the cells to
produce the COVID particle.

00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:25.090
The reason DNA and RNA are the
first vaccines in the clinic

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:28.170
is they're much easier to do

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.970
than isolating the actual
parts of the virus,

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:33.436
so you have DNA and RNA vaccines.

00:19:33.436 --> 00:19:36.470
Another approach, used
by Oxford University,

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:39.890
is to actually have what's
called an attenuated virus,

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:42.510
in this case, the monkey adenovirus.

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:43.900
You weaken that virus

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:47.120
so that it doesn't cause
infection in humans, no illness,

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:49.990
but instead, that virus
is going to express

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:52.960
portions of the COVID-19 virus,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.630
and again, you hope to
create the immune response.

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:59.110
So that's a attenuated
virus-based approach.

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:01.380
And then, I'm trying to simplify

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:03.240
probably a very complex field,

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.030
the other is a so-called subunit virus,

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:10.100
where you actually take
the proteins from COVID-19,

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.800
so you take the surface proteins,

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:15.000
and you combine it with
another thing called adjuvant,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.910
which really boosts immune response,

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:20.240
and you hope that will elicit
a strong immune response.

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:24.210
The subunit-based approach
is largely what we have

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:27.530
for many of the vaccines
people are used to getting,

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:31.400
or the attenuated virus-based approach.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:32.700
Other approaches are you could try

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:35.407
to weaken the COVID-19
virus itself and provide it,

00:20:35.407 --> 00:20:38.120
and so all of these different
things are being approached,

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.653
but the idea of can we
elicit a high antibody titer,

00:20:41.653 --> 00:20:45.310
can we create memory, and importantly,

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:48.283
can we do this in a very,
very, very safe way.

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:50.740
- Right, and make sure I understand.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:52.390
So, at the end of the day,

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.220
you wanna have, essentially,

00:20:54.220 --> 00:20:58.730
a low-risk version, or
cousin version of the virus,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:03.010
or a piece of the virus,
essentially, of a protein,

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:04.610
that your immune system can then

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:07.523
learn to recognize with antibodies,

00:21:07.523 --> 00:21:10.473
and the memory T-cells, and all the rest.

00:21:12.209 --> 00:21:14.200
And, you can do that by,

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:15.120
I mean, you wouldn't insert

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.990
an entire RNA of COVID, would you?

00:21:16.990 --> 00:21:18.700
Or would it be part of the RNA

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:21.431
that would produce some of the proteins?

00:21:21.431 --> 00:21:24.970
- Yes, so we only typically
insert part of the RNA,

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.020
usually a coding RNA
for one of the proteins.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:31.300
It turns out, once RNA
gets beyond a certain size,

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.910
it's very hard to give it in the body,

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:36.310
and have it be stable long enough

00:21:36.310 --> 00:21:37.953
to get to the various cells.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:45.580
- And, what's kind of the worst case?

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:46.590
I mean, not to scare folks,

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:50.270
and I know this isn't
the focus of Novartis,

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:51.290
but you can imagine,

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:53.900
there's a ton of questions
that are coming in

00:21:53.900 --> 00:21:55.190
for how long you think this will last.

00:21:55.190 --> 00:21:56.650
Alina on YouTube was asking, I mean,

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.093
I'm just curious, your views.

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:02.820
- Well, I think from
a broader perspective,

00:22:02.820 --> 00:22:04.080
what's been remarkable to me

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:06.310
is the scale of the medical response.

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:07.470
When you look at it right now,

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:11.290
we have close to 500 vaccines
and drugs in assessment,

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.060
I think close to 5000 clinical trials

00:22:14.060 --> 00:22:17.140
in one stage or another,
over 10,000 publications.

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:20.010
So just like we saw an
exponential increase in the virus,

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.850
we've seen an exponential
increase in the response.

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:25.160
We already know, and I
think it's underestimated,

00:22:25.160 --> 00:22:26.420
how remarkable it is,

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:29.990
we've already discovered
one drug, called Remdesivir,

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:31.750
which is an antiviral drug,

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:35.500
one steroid that the
U.K. has now identified

00:22:35.500 --> 00:22:40.447
that already can improve
patients course with coronavirus.

00:22:40.447 --> 00:22:43.000
I think when many more
of these trials we've got

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.210
of drugs that are being studied,

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:47.120
we have, I think, 12 drugs
being studied right now,

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:50.120
you're gonna get a collection of drugs

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:54.140
that can enable you to reduce
the severity of this disease.

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:57.550
And also, medical practice
is learning so fast.

00:22:57.550 --> 00:22:59.970
So that will hopefully give us some time.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:04.570
Then, I expect the next
hopefully effective therapies

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:06.260
will be so-called antibody therapies.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:11.260
So rather than having the immune
system generate a response,

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:14.180
you can provide patients who get COVID-19

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:16.530
with antibodies that can
hopefully target the virus.

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:19.540
A number of companies working on that.

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:21.660
And all of that, I hope, will,

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.770
and I believe, and maybe I'm an optimist

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:27.400
as a physician/scientist/drug developer,

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:30.144
that all of that will
help us really attenuate

00:23:30.144 --> 00:23:34.230
and mitigate the overall
burden of this pandemic.

00:23:34.230 --> 00:23:35.500
And that will buy us time

00:23:35.500 --> 00:23:37.960
to hopefully get the right vaccine.

00:23:37.960 --> 00:23:41.510
It is worth noting, I've
still yet to develop

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:43.500
a successful vaccine of HIV,

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:45.060
which is an ongoing pandemic

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.390
that started in the early 1980s.

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:50.820
There are many viruses
which I worked for years on,

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:53.880
to try to develop viruses
for something called RSV,

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:56.740
which causes a respiratory illness

00:23:56.740 --> 00:23:58.164
around the world regularly.

00:23:58.164 --> 00:24:00.840
Another virus called cytomegalovirus.

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:03.090
But there's a range of viruses

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.770
that we've been working on for decades

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:07.925
that we were never able to figure out

00:24:07.925 --> 00:24:10.393
how to generate that
strong immune response

00:24:10.393 --> 00:24:12.680
that we've been talking about.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:16.780
And that's just a dose of
realism we have to live with.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:19.340
And drug development
and vaccine development

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:20.423
is not a sure thing.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:24.870
- Yeah, so the hope is the
vaccine might take a year,

00:24:24.870 --> 00:24:26.610
might take 18 months,
it might take two years,

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:28.770
but hopefully we get more Remdesivirs,

00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:30.460
or that steroid-type treatment,

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:33.630
or other things that make it less scary.

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:34.463
- Exactly.
- And this could

00:24:34.463 --> 00:24:35.400
open things up.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:36.700
I mean, when you look at

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:40.423
the general impact on the ecosystem,

00:24:40.423 --> 00:24:41.790
I have this question from YouTube,

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:45.057
Ariana Kristoff-Dullis says,

00:24:45.057 --> 00:24:46.170
"How do you think the pandemic

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:47.810
will affect the long-term future

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:49.020
of the pharmaceutical industry?

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:51.130
Do you think sharing
knowledge and science data

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:52.600
will be more open with in the industry?"

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.850
And I'll add, are there
just manufacturing things

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:57.420
that we're building
capacity to do in the future

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:59.650
that we'll just have
a more rapid response?

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.178
How are you thinking about
that, or what are you seeing?

00:25:02.178 --> 00:25:03.833
- Yeah, I think so, so two
parts to that question.

00:25:03.833 --> 00:25:06.260
I think right now that
the level of collaboration

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:07.110
has been unprecedented.

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:08.834
I co-chair an effort with Bill Gates

00:25:08.834 --> 00:25:10.240
and the Gates Foundation

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:12.460
called the Gates COVID Accelerator.

00:25:12.460 --> 00:25:16.343
There, we have 15 CEOs
really sharing compounds,

00:25:17.655 --> 00:25:21.620
potential drug libraries,
sharing manufacturing capacity.

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:24.320
There's two other big
collaborations also running,

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:26.920
one run by the National
Institutes of Health,

00:25:26.920 --> 00:25:29.110
another informal collaboration.

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:30.980
So there's been a pretty remarkable

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.300
amount of collaboration in the industry.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:34.930
And I think what we're learning from this

00:25:34.930 --> 00:25:37.340
is there are areas in what we call

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:39.920
the so-called pre-competitive space

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:42.072
that I think can be a really
powerful way to accelerate,

00:25:42.072 --> 00:25:45.210
maybe in the future,
discoveries for cancer,

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:48.090
or heart disease, where
if there's something like,

00:25:48.090 --> 00:25:50.010
for instance, one of the
things we collaborate on

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:54.050
is how do you get a good
animal assay for COVID?

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:56.130
So that you know a drug
might work in COVID?

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:59.060
This sounds obvious, but
it's really complicated.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:00.980
And the more we can share our knowledge,

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:02.250
then the more we can-
- And explain what that is,

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:04.173
what is an animal assay?

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:08.930
- So, what we wanna do, is
before we go into humans,

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:12.780
we wanna figure out is a
drug active against COVID-19.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:16.820
So we have to take cells
from an animal or human,

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:18.770
depending on what works best,

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:20.390
and we infect it with COVID,

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:22.230
and we try to treat
the cells with the drug

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:24.560
to see if the drug is active.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:27.670
It turns out you can get lots
of false positives with this,

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:31.380
and how translatable is this into humans?

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.080
And it seems like such a simple thing,

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:36.260
but we've seen amazing
amounts of complexity,

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:38.280
which then could lead you
to spend a lot of time

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:40.840
on drugs that probably don't
make sense to spend time on,

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:43.198
or chase drugs that
should have been killed

00:26:43.198 --> 00:26:45.240
in the development process.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:47.270
So that's one example, a simple example,

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:49.890
of where we're learning we
need to collaborate much more,

00:26:49.890 --> 00:26:52.950
manufacturing capacity is another.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:54.880
So there's a lot of ways, I think,

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:56.820
we're learning we can collaborate.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:58.290
Now I think for future pandemics,

00:26:58.290 --> 00:26:59.620
which I think was part of the question,

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:02.620
I had opportunity to
lead Novartis's response

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:05.580
to the 2009 swine flu pandemic.

00:27:05.580 --> 00:27:08.660
There, Novartis developed
vaccines back then,

00:27:08.660 --> 00:27:11.490
we developed four vaccines
within six months,

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:12.680
but that was influenza,

00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:15.720
and as I mentioned, we would
leverage existing technologies.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:17.280
And we were able to produce, I think,

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:20.481
over 500 million doses
of vaccines and adjutants

00:27:20.481 --> 00:27:22.481
for the world very quickly.

00:27:22.481 --> 00:27:26.150
And it's interesting, we know
what's required, I think,

00:27:26.150 --> 00:27:27.560
to really manage pandemics.

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:30.780
First, there's a series of
locations around the world,

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.000
which are well-understood,

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:35.120
where we know animals and humans interact,

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:37.520
and there's the possibility for viruses

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:38.860
to move to animals and humans.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:42.960
They're called zoonotic
hotspots, they're well-mapped,

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:45.850
and we can really do
surveillance on these sites

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:48.010
if we make the relevant investment.

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:50.150
Then we need to have a lot of transparency

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:53.130
to rapidly know when something
has jumped into humans

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:54.867
so that we don't lose time.

00:27:54.867 --> 00:27:57.120
Then we need two other things,

00:27:57.120 --> 00:28:00.060
we need warm capacity, we call it warm,

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:03.620
it's a ready capacity for
vaccine and drug production

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:04.960
that could be rapidly scaled up.

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:06.310
That's one of the challenges right now

00:28:06.310 --> 00:28:09.060
is we're trying to find
enough manufacturing capacity.

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:10.680
And we need public health systems

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:12.450
that are prepared for these kind of

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:14.920
very rare high-risk events.

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:16.950
And that's hard, you have to store

00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:20.810
personal protective
equipment, and other things,

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:22.920
for something that may never come.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:25.169
But when it does come, you're ready,

00:28:25.169 --> 00:28:26.050
you have stockpiles ready,

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:27.890
and you can rapidly deploy them.

00:28:27.890 --> 00:28:30.110
These were the findings,
I think, after SARS,

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:31.530
these were the findings after swine flu,

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:33.370
I suspect these will be the findings

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:34.620
after this next pandemic.

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:38.670
- That's super-valuable, and you know,

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:40.010
when we're having these
interesting conversations,

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:41.180
I completely lose track of time.

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:44.453
I realized we're already out of time.

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:48.824
Any finishing thoughts?

00:28:48.824 --> 00:28:51.160
There's a bunch of
questions about young people

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:52.930
who are interested in
career tracks, you know,

00:28:52.930 --> 00:28:54.380
entering into the pharmaceutical industry.

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:57.360
Obviously you all's
support for Khan Academy

00:28:57.360 --> 00:28:59.410
is in that spirit of how do we have

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:01.090
more people be able to enter

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:05.530
this clearly very important
industry in the world?

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:07.680
Any finishing advice for young people

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:11.203
thinking about careers in
pharmaceuticals or biotech?

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:15.750
- Yeah, I think the
future in our industry,

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:16.620
like many industries,

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:19.980
is gonna be continuing to marry
different sectors together.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:22.290
So, I think drug discovery today

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:24.900
is not gonna be what drug
discovery is tomorrow.

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:27.530
I would encourage all of
the students listening

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:31.740
to learn how to marry data
science and technology

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:34.330
with biology and chemistry,

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:35.930
because so much now of our work

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:37.250
is shifting in that direction,

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:39.071
how can you leverage technology

00:29:39.071 --> 00:29:43.490
to really speed up a lot of these efforts.

00:29:43.490 --> 00:29:46.270
I would say, build up,
also, as I said before,

00:29:46.270 --> 00:29:49.000
your range of understanding
across different disciplines,

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.040
because you don't know where
the world will take you.

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:55.320
I never wanted to be a
CEO, but yet, here I am.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:59.580
And so, I think, building
range in yourself can help,

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:01.220
and that includes, I
mean, I'm a big believer

00:30:01.220 --> 00:30:03.550
in books and philosophy,

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:06.350
so I think all of those
things can really matter.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.100
And then maybe, my last comment,
Sal, I just wanted to add,

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:11.610
is I believe in this moment in time

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:14.590
where we wanna build a more equal society,

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:19.300
where we tackle topics like
structural inequalities

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:22.200
and the problem of race
in the United States,

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:25.220
I think Khan Academy has
a remarkable role to play.

00:30:25.220 --> 00:30:26.810
We know, fundamentally,

00:30:26.810 --> 00:30:29.970
the educational system can be empowered

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:32.340
by great technology like Khan Academy.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:34.420
So, I learned that,

00:30:34.420 --> 00:30:37.970
I read a book called "Savage
Inequalities" in 1994,

00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:39.438
talking about the inequities

00:30:39.438 --> 00:30:42.347
in educational systems
of the United States,

00:30:42.347 --> 00:30:45.290
and I firmly believe Khan
Academy helps tackle that,

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:47.210
so we're proud to be a supporter,

00:30:47.210 --> 00:30:49.373
and look forward to the collaboration.

00:30:50.770 --> 00:30:53.373
- Oh, appreciate that,
and obviously, we agree.

00:30:55.014 --> 00:30:57.990
Well, Vas, thanks so
much for taking time out.

00:30:57.990 --> 00:31:00.070
I'm sure, I can only
imagine how busy you are,

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:01.180
so this really means a lot.

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:03.293
Thank you for your support, and really,

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:06.040
why aren't you on the news channels

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:07.670
explaining about vaccines?

00:31:07.670 --> 00:31:10.120
Because I feel like we're not
getting that level of depth,

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:11.970
so someone needs to give you
a call, if you have the time.

00:31:11.970 --> 00:31:13.560
- Yeah, maybe, maybe.

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:15.203
I think maybe I'm too realistic.

00:31:16.416 --> 00:31:20.548
- Maybe you're saying things
they don't wanna hear.

00:31:20.548 --> 00:31:22.480
Well, thanks so much, Vas,

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:24.470
and I hope we get a
chance to do this again.

00:31:24.470 --> 00:31:25.303
This was a real treat.

00:31:25.303 --> 00:31:26.136
- Absolutely, take care, Sal,

00:31:26.136 --> 00:31:28.312
and thank you everyone for listening.

00:31:28.312 --> 00:31:29.473
- Thank you.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:33.660
Well, everyone, well,
yesterday someone asked

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:35.520
what do I learn from these live streams,

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:36.353
or what have I learned?

00:31:36.353 --> 00:31:37.710
I think that was just another good example

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.630
where, on a book level,

00:31:40.630 --> 00:31:42.150
and obviously I've taught on Khan Academy

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:44.920
things about immunology, and antibodies,

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:48.590
and T-cells, but just talking to someone

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:50.120
who's a real practitioner in the field,

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:51.360
who's worked on vaccines,

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:53.880
who runs one of the largest
pharmaceutical companies

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:54.930
in the world, you know,

00:31:54.930 --> 00:31:56.150
you just learned a whole new level

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:57.120
of what's going on in the world.

00:31:57.120 --> 00:31:59.763
So hopefully you enjoyed
that as much as I did.

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:02.310
There's some things to ponder,

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:04.500
if this whole pandemic
goes a little bit longer

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:06.032
than we might have expected,

00:32:06.032 --> 00:32:06.947
but hopefully you enjoyed that,

00:32:06.947 --> 00:32:08.860
and I look forward to,

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:10.470
I'm not sure if we're doing
it tomorrow or the day after,

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:12.320
I need to look at my schedule.

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:13.753
I live my life hour-to-hour.

00:32:15.668 --> 00:32:18.520
But I will see you all later
this week, at the homeroom.

00:32:18.520 --> 00:32:19.410
See you soon.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:20.943
Stay safe and healthy.

